From: Portal <116828772+Retrorerr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] backends/anland: Apply pending input before a select

The consumer writes a vsync's input to the data socket before it selects
the buffer for that vsync, but KWin watches the socket and the
buffer-ready eventfd with separate notifiers. When both are ready, Qt can
dispatch buffer-ready first, so the frame callbacks of the frame it
schedules reach clients ahead of that vsync's input. A client that
samples input at its frame callback (Qt Quick, Gecko) then shows no
motion for one frame and twice the motion in the next: about one frame
in twelve during a touch drag. Read all input already written before
handling a select.
---
 src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp b/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
index ffffe35..552281d 100644
--- a/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
+++ b/src/backends/anland/anland_backend.cpp
@@ -518,6 +518,18 @@ void AnlandBackend::onBufferReady()
         return;
     }
 
+    // The consumer writes a vsync's input before it selects the buffer for
+    // that vsync, but the input socket and this eventfd are separate
+    // notifiers: when both are ready, Qt may dispatch this one first, and the
+    // frame callbacks of the frame it schedules then reach clients before the
+    // input does. A client that samples input at its frame callback (Qt Quick,
+    // Gecko) shows no motion for that frame and twice the motion in the next.
+    // Applying everything already written first makes the order explicit.
+    onInputReadable();
+    if (m_inFallback) {
+        return;
+    }
+
     // The dmabuf set is already in hand (try_exit_fallback() received it when we
     // left fallback), so buffer-ready is purely a frame-completion signal: drain
     // the eventfd and pace the render cycle.
